if it isn't too late.

255
00:10:33,366 --> 00:10:34,767
- The guy's a crowd-pleaser.

256
00:10:36,068 --> 00:10:38,471
- You talk as if
he's still alive.

257
00:10:38,471 --> 00:10:39,438
- [Doctor] He is.

258
00:10:39,438 --> 00:10:40,706
- [Mike] He's only mostly dead.

259
00:10:40,706 --> 00:10:43,342
- [Doctor] I just picked
up a faint pulse beat.

260
00:10:43,342 --> 00:10:45,878
- Keep massaging the heart.
- I am, I am.

261
00:10:45,878 --> 00:10:47,413
- [Tom] Oh, that feels good.

262
00:10:47,413 --> 00:10:49,348
- An electric shock should
stimulate the motor area

263
00:10:49,348 --> 00:10:51,317
enough to enervate
the heart again.

264
00:10:51,317 --> 00:10:53,986
Then he won't need
any external stimuli.

265
00:10:53,986 --> 00:10:56,355
- Keep away from the motor area.

266
00:10:56,355 --> 00:10:58,424
You'll paralyze him for good.

267
00:10:58,424 --> 00:11:00,726
- Which would you rather
be, paralyzed or dead?

268
00:11:00,726 --> 00:11:02,061
- [You're trying to play God.

269
00:11:02,061 --> 00:11:03,663
- [Tom] Tag, You're God.

270
00:11:03,663 --> 00:11:05,531
- Not yours to make.

271
00:11:05,531 --> 00:11:07,199
- When the obstetrician has
to decide which to save,

272
00:11:07,199 --> 00:11:09,201
the mother or the child,
who plays God then?

273
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:10,603
- [Mike] He's pregnant too?

274
00:11:10,603 --> 00:11:11,604
- It's part of the game.

275
00:11:11,604 --> 00:11:13,205
- The game?

276
00:11:13,205 --> 00:11:16,175
The human body's not a jigsaw
puzzle to experiment on.

277
00:11:16,175 --> 00:11:17,543
- It's Gnip Gnop.

278
00:11:17,543 --> 00:11:19,111
- You're playing it safe
like the other doctors.

279
00:11:19,111 --> 00:11:20,713
- [Mike] Yeah, jagoff.

280
00:11:20,713 --> 00:11:23,282
- I'll save my breath.
Keep massaging the heart.

281
00:11:23,282 --> 00:11:25,051
You've already lost
your patient, doctor.

282
00:11:25,051 --> 00:11:26,252
I'm going to save mine.

283
00:11:26,252 --> 00:11:27,687
- [Tom] Ooh, that stung.

284
00:11:27,687 --> 00:11:29,288
- [Doctor] The pulse is coming
back stronger than ever.

285
00:11:29,288 --> 00:11:30,489
- Thank you, Grandma.

286
00:11:30,489 --> 00:11:32,024
- [Doctor] Unbelievable.

287
00:11:32,024 --> 00:11:33,225
- Nothing is unbelievable

288
00:11:33,225 --> 00:11:35,361
if you have the
nerve to experiment.

289
00:11:35,361 --> 00:11:38,397
I've been working on
something like this for weeks

290
00:11:38,397 --> 00:11:39,965
- In your laboratory?

291
00:11:39,965 --> 00:11:41,534
- [Mike] Yes, my laboratory.

292
00:11:41,534 --> 00:11:43,602
- I knew this would work if
only I had the opportunity.

293
00:11:43,602 --> 00:11:46,305
- We don't conduct
experiments on people.

294
00:11:46,305 --> 00:11:47,973
You should be sure
of the results.

295
00:11:47,973 --> 00:11:49,575
- Then they wouldn't
be experiments.

296
00:11:49,575 --> 00:11:51,210
- [Tom] Yeah.

297
00:11:51,210 --> 00:11:54,814
- I am now. Stop
massaging the heart.

298
00:11:54,814 --> 00:11:56,449
- [Tom] You were
enjoying it too much.

299
00:11:56,449 --> 00:11:58,317
- Let's see if it can
take over by itself.

300
00:12:00,252 --> 00:12:01,821
- Yep, I'll take it from here.

301
00:12:05,624 --> 00:12:09,729
- [Mike] Luke, join me or you'll
star in "Corvette Summer".

302
00:12:12,865 --> 00:12:15,634
- All right, close up the chest.

303
00:12:15,634 --> 00:12:17,136
- [Tom] Zip!

304
00:12:17,136 --> 00:12:19,371
- I'm about finished
with the cerebral area.

305
00:12:19,371 --> 00:12:21,474
- [Mike] Oh, leave
his area alone.

306
00:12:21,474 --> 00:12:22,675
- How's his pulse?

307
00:12:22,675 --> 00:12:24,143
- [Doctor] Strong and steady.

308
00:12:24,143 --> 00:12:26,011
- Oh, it's real strong
and steady, don't ya know.

309
00:12:28,681 --> 00:12:30,282
- [Tom] Geez, what
the hell is this?

310
00:12:30,282 --> 00:12:33,085
A long one, boy. Stringy too.

311
00:12:36,088 --> 00:12:38,124
- [Mike] There, put
your finger right there.

312
00:12:40,259 --> 00:12:42,962
- I'm giving him a little
piping. Think it looks nice.

313
00:12:44,764 --> 00:12:46,398
- Hey, you did
perform a miracle.

314
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:49,969
I may not approve
of your method, but.

315
00:12:49,969 --> 00:12:51,337
- [Tom] The fans
seem to like it.

316
00:12:51,337 --> 00:12:52,772
- I am proud of your results.

317
00:12:58,277 --> 00:12:59,545
- [Mike] Anyway,
I can't find it.

318
00:12:59,545 --> 00:13:01,447
I think my class
ring's in, in him.

319
00:13:01,447 --> 00:13:03,649
- Don't worry.
We'll get it later.

320
00:13:03,649 --> 00:13:06,452
- [Tom] Geez, someone should
have invented the IV stand.

321
00:13:08,187 --> 00:13:11,123
- [Mike] Then take it
easy in the hallways.

322
00:13:11,123 --> 00:13:13,425
- [Tom] And so the little
fellow was wheeled into post-op

323
00:13:13,425 --> 00:13:14,627
for close observation.

324
00:13:16,295 --> 00:13:17,596
- I, I love you, Dad.

325
00:13:17,596 --> 00:13:18,831
- I love you, Son.

326
00:13:23,435 --> 00:13:25,805
- [Mike] Hey, you're not
my dad! You're Hitler!

327
00:13:27,139 --> 00:13:30,843
- An amazing, even an
extraordinary operation, son.

328
00:13:32,545 --> 00:13:35,114
Still, it's too risky,
too uncontrolled.

329
00:13:35,114 --> 00:13:36,715
- [Tom] Oh, don't
start, old man.

330
00:13:36,715 --> 00:13:37,650
- I saved his life.

331
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:42,054
- And the aftereffects?
What about them?

332
00:13:42,054 --> 00:13:43,355
- What about them? They're fun!

333
00:13:43,355 --> 00:13:46,091
- You've lost the
urge to experiment.

334
00:13:46,091 --> 00:13:46,992
To explore.

335
00:13:48,394 --> 00:13:50,863
- [Mike] Order up. I need
those onion rings on the fly.

336
00:13:52,965 --> 00:13:53,933
- [Tom] You know,
they're doctors,

337
00:13:53,933 --> 00:13:55,668
but they do puppet shows too.

338
00:13:55,668 --> 00:13:56,702
- Oh, Judy!

339
00:13:56,702 --> 00:13:59,071
- Don't explore on people.

340
00:13:59,071 --> 00:14:01,106
Before you put a scalpel to one,

341
00:14:01,106 --> 00:14:03,509
an operation like this needs
testing under every condition.

342
00:14:03,509 --> 00:14:04,743
- Yeah. You want mayo?

343
00:14:04,743 --> 00:14:08,948
- Over and over again.
Rabbits, mice, monkeys.

344
00:14:08,948 --> 00:14:09,782
- [Mike] Humpty-back camels.

345
00:14:09,782 --> 00:14:11,083
- Not people.

346
00:14:11,083 --> 00:14:13,352
- That man who should be
dead now won't think so.

347
00:14:13,352 --> 00:14:15,054
- What do you say to that, huh?

348
00:14:15,054 --> 00:14:16,689
- There's more to
surgery than just being

349
00:14:16,689 --> 00:14:20,326
a carpenter to patch up walls
or a plumber or drain pipes.

350
00:14:20,326 --> 00:14:21,961
- Or a muffin baker
to bake muffins.

351
00:14:21,961 --> 00:14:23,562
- Our bodies are
capable of adjusting

352
00:14:23,562 --> 00:14:25,130
in ways we've hardly dreamt of.

353
00:14:25,130 --> 00:14:26,432
- [Mike] Can we not talk shop?

354
00:14:26,432 --> 00:14:28,167
- If we can only find the key.

355
00:14:29,335 --> 00:14:32,004
I'm so close now, so very close.

356
00:14:32,004 --> 00:14:34,039
- You're different, son.

357
00:14:34,039 --> 00:14:35,441
- The key to what?

358
00:14:35,441 --> 00:14:37,042
- Complete transplantations.

359
00:14:38,143 --> 00:14:40,179
To be able to transplant
limbs and organs.

360
00:14:40,179 --> 00:14:41,780
- [Mike] To boldly
go where no man-

361
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:43,849
- To be able to replace
diseased and damaged

362
00:14:43,849 --> 00:14:46,952
parts of the body as easily
as we replace eye corneas now.

363
00:14:46,952 --> 00:14:48,621
- [Tom] We'll call
em Snap-Tite Limbs.

364
00:14:48,621 --> 00:14:50,155
- So that the new parts
will join together

365
00:14:50,155 --> 00:14:51,357
as though they were born there.

366
00:14:51,357 --> 00:14:52,858
- [Tom] And rule the world!

367
00:14:52,858 --> 00:14:54,226
- It can't be done.

368
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:56,962
- It can be done.

369
00:14:56,962 --> 00:14:57,796
- Can't!
- Can!

370
00:14:57,796 --> 00:14:59,131
- Can't!
- Can!

371
00:14:59,131 --> 00:15:00,099
- Can't can't can't can't!
- Can can can!

372
00:15:00,099 --> 00:15:02,601
(upbeat music)

373
00:15:33,232 --> 00:15:35,701
- With my new special compound
I've created, I'll do it.

374
00:15:35,701 --> 00:15:37,036
I know I can do it.

375
00:15:37,036 --> 00:15:38,003
- Sure, sure, that's
what you think.

376
00:15:38,003 --> 00:15:39,505
- That's what I know.

377
00:15:39,505 --> 00:15:40,606
I know I'm close.

378
00:15:43,242 --> 00:15:44,543
- [Mike] What do you want?

379
00:15:45,678 --> 00:15:48,547
- I'm so proud of
you I could kiss you.

380
00:15:48,547 --> 00:15:49,815
- [Tom] And your name is?

381
00:15:49,815 --> 00:15:51,717
- Always promises.

382
00:15:51,717 --> 00:15:53,953
- Careful. Your father's
liable to report us.

383
00:15:54,820 --> 00:15:55,921
- Stop the floor show?

384
00:15:57,323 --> 00:15:58,824
When you to a married, it
won't be fun to watch anymore.

385
00:15:58,824 --> 00:16:01,827
- Ooh, well, that's late night.

386
00:16:01,827 --> 00:16:03,062
- Your grandchildren
won't be test tube babies.

387
00:16:03,062 --> 00:16:04,229
- What does that mean?

388
00:16:04,229 --> 00:16:05,164
- You'd better
hurry if you want to

389
00:16:05,164 --> 00:16:06,565
catch that plane to Denver, Dad.

390
00:16:06,565 --> 00:16:09,001
You know that medical convention
can't start without you.

391
00:16:09,001 --> 00:16:10,135
- [Mike] You're the MC.

392
00:16:11,537 --> 00:16:13,772
- Jan, you'd better check
about my reservations.

393
00:16:13,772 --> 00:16:15,174
- Oh yes. I'll call the airport.

394
00:16:15,174 --> 00:16:16,108
I'll be right back.

395
00:16:17,343 --> 00:16:19,378
- [Tom] She's red hot, son.

396
00:16:19,378 --> 00:16:20,412
- Bill.

397
00:16:20,412 --> 00:16:21,680
- [Tom] Honey.

398
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,582
- The line between
scientific genius

399
00:16:23,582 --> 00:16:26,151
and obsessive fanaticism
is a thin one.

400
00:16:26,151 --> 00:16:27,987
Now, I want you
on the right side.

401
00:16:27,987 --> 00:16:29,254
- If I don't experiment,

402
00:16:29,254 --> 00:16:30,489
how can I hope to
perform operations

403
00:16:30,489 --> 00:16:32,191
like the one you
almost messed up.

404
00:16:32,191 --> 00:16:34,426
- But I can't cover
up for you any more.

405
00:16:34,426 --> 00:16:37,096
The superintendent
had it out with me.

406
00:16:37,096 --> 00:16:39,198
He thinks it's you who has
been stealing those limbs

407
00:16:39,198 --> 00:16:41,500
from the amputee operations.

408
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:42,735
- So what if it is?

409
00:16:42,735 --> 00:16:44,269
- [Mike] That's
mildly unethical.

410
00:16:44,269 --> 00:16:45,838
I've got to have limbs for
my transplant experiments.

411
00:16:45,838 --> 00:16:47,272
- [Tom] They're delicious.

412
00:16:47,272 --> 00:16:48,707
- Well, you said test and
experiment, test and experiment.

413
00:16:48,707 --> 00:16:51,744
- Yes, but limbs and
organs taken from people?

414
00:16:51,744 --> 00:16:54,079
- I've got to have
them to work with.

415
00:16:54,079 --> 00:16:55,981
- Come on, give me the
keys to the morgue, Dad.

416
00:16:55,981 --> 00:16:57,816
- Sure, I've made
a few mistakes.

417
00:16:57,816 --> 00:16:59,018
- [Tom] Oops.

418
00:16:59,018 --> 00:17:00,619
- But I've learned from
them. I've learned.

419
00:17:00,619 --> 00:17:02,254
- [Mike] So you've
learned, then.

420
00:17:04,723 --> 00:17:07,793
- Your reservations are
all set. 3:30 take-off.

421
00:17:07,793 --> 00:17:09,461
- [Tom] Anything else?

422
00:17:09,461 --> 00:17:12,831
- Well, what have you two
planned for the weekend?

423
00:17:12,831 --> 00:17:13,799
- Oh, nothing much.

424
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:16,635
A quiet weekend.

425
00:17:16,635 --> 00:17:18,270
- You're sure you're not
going up to the country house?

426
00:17:18,270 --> 00:17:19,671
- We have a country house?

427
00:17:19,671 --> 00:17:21,540
- You're always
sneaking off up there.

428
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:24,043
The place gives me the creeps.

429
00:17:24,043 --> 00:17:25,677
I should've sold it
when your mother died.

430
00:17:25,677 --> 00:17:26,779
- You can't sell that place.

431
00:17:26,779 --> 00:17:28,313
- [Mike] Wait, Mother's dead?

432
00:17:28,313 --> 00:17:29,481
- Well, I mean, it's nice
to get away from the city.

433
00:17:29,481 --> 00:17:31,216
I can work without
anyone snooping around.

434
00:17:31,216 --> 00:17:32,351
- You spend too
much time up there.

435
00:17:32,351 --> 00:17:33,185
- [Tom] Yeah.

436
00:17:34,453 --> 00:17:37,656
- All right, I'll see
you both in a few days.

437
00:17:37,656 --> 00:17:39,191
I have to clean up
and get out of here.

438
00:17:39,191 --> 00:17:40,125
- [Tom] And I don't want you

439
00:17:40,125 --> 00:17:42,261
reanimating anything, young man.

440
00:17:42,261 --> 00:17:44,496
- Fine operation or not, Bill,

441
00:17:44,496 --> 00:17:45,831
you're walking on thin ice.

442
00:17:46,665 --> 00:17:48,000
Don't go too far.

443
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,035
- And don't cry
over spilled milk.

444
00:17:50,035 --> 00:17:51,837
And don't count your
chicks before they hatch.

445
00:17:51,837 --> 00:17:53,005
I'm outta here.

446
00:17:53,005 --> 00:17:55,007
- [Mike] Your
dad's such a boner.

447
00:17:55,007 --> 00:17:58,710
- Oh, every time you touch
me, I go out of my mind.

448
00:17:58,710 --> 00:18:00,712
- [Tom] But please
wash your hands.

449
00:18:00,712 --> 00:18:02,247
- [Mike] Tissue, all right.

450
00:18:02,247 --> 00:18:05,217
- Bill, I want to get married.
I can't stand not having you.

451
00:18:05,217 --> 00:18:07,286
- You've been wonderful.

452
00:18:07,286 --> 00:18:08,587
- I'd rather be a bride.

453
00:18:09,721 --> 00:18:10,856
- In a few more weeks.

454
00:18:12,291 --> 00:18:13,659
Nothing will keep us apart.

455
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:16,295
We'll be together.

456
00:18:16,295 --> 00:18:17,830
- Doodly doodly
doodly, we're apart!

457
00:18:19,364 --> 00:18:21,467
- [Mike] Oh, you taste
like Vince Edwards.

458
00:18:23,969 --> 00:18:25,571
- Sylvia Plath, RN.

459
00:18:27,106 --> 00:18:28,507
- [Tom] (whistling) Taxi!

460
00:18:28,507 --> 00:18:29,708
- Dr. Cortner!

461
00:18:29,708 --> 00:18:32,244
- Could you pee in
the bell jar, please?

462
00:18:32,244 --> 00:18:33,078
- [Mike] Whoa.

463
00:18:34,947 --> 00:18:36,014
- I'm so glad you're here.

464
00:18:36,014 --> 00:18:37,850
I was afraid you'd gotten away.

465
00:18:37,850 --> 00:18:40,452
There was a very important
phone message that came for you.

466
00:18:40,452 --> 00:18:42,921
It sounded quite urgent.
I've been looking everywhere.

467
00:18:42,921 --> 00:18:45,023
It was from a man called Kurt.

468
00:18:45,023 --> 00:18:47,493
He called from
the country place,

469
00:18:47,493 --> 00:18:49,595
and he said something
terrible has happened.

470
00:18:49,595 --> 00:18:51,196
He wanted you to come right out.

471
00:18:52,197 --> 00:18:53,432
- Thank you.

472
00:18:53,432 --> 00:18:55,067
- [Mike] But I'm too
cool for emergencies.

473
00:18:57,069 --> 00:18:58,303
- You've always wanted to know

474
00:18:58,303 --> 00:19:00,239
what's kept me away from
you so many weekends.

475
00:19:01,406 --> 00:19:02,541
Have you got the
keys to your car?

476
00:19:03,709 --> 00:19:06,945
- The answer? Yes, she
has the keys to the car.

477
00:19:08,347 --> 00:19:11,116
- [Tom] The long, long trailer.

478
00:19:11,116 --> 00:19:13,952
- [Mike] Sid and
Nancy, American style.

479
00:19:13,952 --> 00:19:16,655
- Why haven't you ever
taken me up here before?

480
00:19:16,655 --> 00:19:18,724
- Because the things I'm
working on don't need audience.

481
00:19:18,724 --> 00:19:19,925
- [Tom] It's performance art.

482
00:19:19,925 --> 00:19:22,461
- That telephone
call. What about it?

483
00:19:22,461 --> 00:19:25,230
- All right, all right,
hold off the questions.

484
00:19:25,230 --> 00:19:27,032
- Why the mystery, Bill?

485
00:19:27,032 --> 00:19:28,534
What's this all about?

486
00:19:29,701 --> 00:19:31,436
- We'll be there soon
enough. You'll see.

487
00:19:31,436 --> 00:19:32,871
- [Mike] Now shut up,
you're my trophy wife.

488
00:19:32,871 --> 00:19:34,473
- I've got to hurry.

489
00:19:34,473 --> 00:19:36,475
(suspenseful music)

490
00:19:36,475 --> 00:19:38,110
- Chukkas to the floor, hurry!

491
00:19:39,878 --> 00:19:42,147
- [Tom] Nick
Mancuso is Stingray.

492
00:19:44,483 --> 00:19:46,652
- [Mike] A sign lift
by ancient astronauts.

493
00:19:48,253 --> 00:19:50,856
(suspenseful music)

494
00:19:50,856 --> 00:19:52,691
- You will enjoy
the tight suspension

495
00:19:52,691 --> 00:19:53,792
of the Mercury Comet.

496
00:19:57,629 --> 00:19:59,631
- [Mike] Think I'll
have a Papa Burger. You?

497
00:20:01,366 --> 00:20:03,068
- Man, I want a pair
of those Chukkas.

498
00:20:04,403 --> 00:20:06,205
- [Tom] And can we stop
at Grandma Squeakers'?

499
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:07,773
Can we stop at Turks' Inn?
- No.

500
00:20:07,773 --> 00:20:08,807
- [Tom] Can we stop
at the Big Steer?

501
00:20:08,807 --> 00:20:10,042
- [Mike] No.

502
00:20:10,042 --> 00:20:10,976
- [Tom] Can we stop
at Cranberry Cove?

503
00:20:10,976 --> 00:20:13,178
- Is he chasing James Dean?

504
00:20:13,178 --> 00:20:15,047
- [Mike] Wow. That
was my prom theme.

505
00:20:18,951 --> 00:20:20,919
- [Tom] What's this?

506
00:20:20,919 --> 00:20:22,854
Our fanatical physician
and his fickle fiance

507
00:20:22,854 --> 00:20:25,224
e speeding to their
country cottage?

508
00:20:26,124 --> 00:20:27,659
- [Mike] That's pretty good.

509
00:20:27,659 --> 00:20:31,063
- Mrs. Webb, you're doing very
well. Careful now, Mrs. Webb.

510
00:20:31,063 --> 00:20:32,931
You're over the center
line, Mrs. Webb.

511
00:20:34,433 --> 00:20:35,801
- [Mike] I've got to hurry.

512
00:20:37,536 --> 00:20:39,271
- [Tom] Stop sign,
what stop sign?

513
00:20:39,271 --> 00:20:40,439
Curve, what curve?

514
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:43,942
- Ah, the road is attacking me!

515
00:20:46,178 --> 00:20:47,946
- [Mike] We now join
"Barefoot in the Park"

516
00:20:47,946 --> 00:20:49,281
already in progress.

517
00:20:50,482 --> 00:20:52,451
- [Tom] Look out,
look out, look out!

518
00:20:52,451 --> 00:20:55,053
♪ Leader of the pack

519
00:20:55,053 --> 00:20:58,123
- Oh man, I'm really tired
from the crash. Honey?

520
00:20:58,123 --> 00:21:01,827
Honey?

521
00:21:01,827 --> 00:21:03,395
- [Mike] Well, this
shouldn't be too,

522
00:21:03,395 --> 00:21:05,897
oh, honey, what are you doing?

523
00:21:06,932 --> 00:21:07,766
Ew.

524
00:21:11,770 --> 00:21:15,073
- [Tom] Diarrhea is like
a storm raging inside you.

525
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:20,112
- Shouldn't have eaten
at Jack-in-the-Box.

526
00:21:26,818 --> 00:21:28,220
- [Mike] Hi, I'm
William Proxmire.

527
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:29,721
How are you doing?
Put her there.

528
00:21:32,958 --> 00:21:35,127
- [Tom] (sniffing) It
actually smells good.

529
00:21:36,595 --> 00:21:38,864
- [Mike] Honey
roasted. Honey roasted?

530
00:21:38,864 --> 00:21:41,300
(Tom laughing)

531
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:43,869
- Here, put this on.
It'll keep you warm.

532
00:21:43,869 --> 00:21:45,570
- [Mike] Would you
give me that back?

533
00:21:45,570 --> 00:21:47,239
It makes you look tall.

534
00:21:49,241 --> 00:21:50,342
- [Tom] Is that what, oh!

535
00:21:50,342 --> 00:21:53,178
- Ew!
- That's her head!

536
00:21:53,178 --> 00:21:54,946
- [Tom] Ooh, yuck.

537
00:21:54,946 --> 00:21:58,016
Don't forget my purse! Honey!

538
00:21:58,016 --> 00:21:59,651
- The rest of it's
pretty crispy.

539
00:22:03,955 --> 00:22:06,358
- [Tom] You just had
to go fast, didn't you?

540
00:22:06,358 --> 00:22:08,927
Well look, Mr. Big Shot. Now
I don't have a body anymore.

541
00:22:08,927 --> 00:22:09,961
Are you happy?

542
00:22:12,364 --> 00:22:14,933
- [Mike] Guys, I remember
his fiancee as being taller.

543
00:22:14,933 --> 00:22:15,767
- Yeah.
- Yep.

544
00:22:18,003 --> 00:22:18,937
- Thank you, God.

545
00:22:18,937 --> 00:22:20,739
Thank you so bloody much.

546
00:22:23,608 --> 00:22:26,311
- [Tom] I just realized what
I did. It's really gross.

547
00:22:35,253 --> 00:22:37,055
- [Mike] Briggens is at
the 20, he's at the 10.

548
00:22:37,055 --> 00:22:38,523
No one will catch him.

549
00:22:38,523 --> 00:22:40,058
- It's either gonna
win the Nobel Prize

550
00:22:40,058 --> 00:22:41,193
or the Heisman Trophy.

551
00:22:42,594 --> 00:22:43,929
- [Tom] What if people
get the wrong idea?

552
00:22:43,929 --> 00:22:46,231
This could really
be taken wrong.

553
00:22:46,231 --> 00:22:47,833
Gotta get outta here.

554
00:22:47,833 --> 00:22:49,534
- [Mike] Hi doctor. How
are you doing there?

555
00:22:49,534 --> 00:22:50,802
- Oh, no problem. No head.

556
00:23:07,953 --> 00:23:10,088
- Oh, hey Cambot, bring
it down here, okay?

557
00:23:10,088 --> 00:23:10,922
Come on, buddy.

558
00:23:12,324 --> 00:23:13,625
- Just yank it out. Don't
worry about the wires.

559
00:23:13,625 --> 00:23:15,060
- He doesn't know
what he's doing.

560
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:16,294
You don't know what
you're doing, do ya, Mike?

561
00:23:16,294 --> 00:23:18,397
- You guys, I've been
studying the manuals

562
00:23:18,397 --> 00:23:20,132
ever since I got here.

563
00:23:20,132 --> 00:23:21,600
I figure if we can gain control

564
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:22,834
of the inertial guidance system,

565
00:23:22,834 --> 00:23:25,303
we got a chance of
getting out of here.

566
00:23:25,303 --> 00:23:26,204
Anybody know what this does?

567
00:23:26,204 --> 00:23:27,239
- Uh, nope.

568
00:23:27,239 --> 00:23:28,507
- No, no way.

569
00:23:28,507 --> 00:23:29,875
I don't think it's
important anyway.

570
00:23:29,875 --> 00:23:31,109
- Just get rid of it then.

571
00:23:31,109 --> 00:23:32,577
- Just cut the big
red cable. Try that.

572
00:23:32,577 --> 00:23:34,446
- So why do you want to leave
so bad? You just got here.

573
00:23:34,446 --> 00:23:36,815
- [Mike] You've been watching
the same movie I have, right?

574
00:23:36,815 --> 00:23:38,383
A man's gotta try, fellas.

575
00:23:38,383 --> 00:23:41,920
Hey, guys. It's
a mess down here.

576
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:44,790
There's old sandwiches,
Tom, you got like one, two,

577
00:23:44,790 --> 00:23:47,592
no, there's like four heads
down here, did you know that?

578
00:23:47,592 --> 00:23:48,794
- Sure.

579
00:23:48,794 --> 00:23:50,829
- There's a dozen copies
of "The Picardian".

580
00:23:50,829 --> 00:23:53,331
- Hey, those are mine. Those
are gonna be valuable one day.

581
00:23:53,331 --> 00:23:55,033
- The big red cable,
Mike, just cut it.

582
00:23:55,033 --> 00:23:57,169
- [Mike] Which one?
There's about 20.

583
00:23:57,169 --> 00:23:59,070
- Um, the green one.

584
00:23:59,070 --> 00:23:59,938
- Okay.

585
00:24:03,275 --> 00:24:04,276
- Gee, boy.

586
00:24:04,276 --> 00:24:05,911
Oh boy. Gee.

587
00:24:05,911 --> 00:24:08,947
That's my fault. That was
the cheese compressor line.

588
00:24:08,947 --> 00:24:10,449
Sorry.

589
00:24:10,449 --> 00:24:11,950
- You guys,

590
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:13,852
I don't think we're gonna
gain control from here.

591
00:24:13,852 --> 00:24:14,853
- Of course we're not.

592
00:24:14,853 --> 00:24:15,887
- Nah, heck no.

593
00:24:15,887 --> 00:24:17,355
We tried that a year ago.

594
00:24:17,355 --> 00:24:20,525
- Well, why didn't you tell
me? Geez, wasted all this time.

595
00:24:20,525 --> 00:24:21,460
- No, Mike, 'cause.

596
00:24:21,460 --> 00:24:22,994
- 'Cause if you find a way out,

597
00:24:22,994 --> 00:24:24,930
then you'll go and you'll
leave us all alone up here

598
00:24:24,930 --> 00:24:26,131
and well, you'll be gone.

599
00:24:26,131 --> 00:24:27,599
- Yeah.

600
00:24:27,599 --> 00:24:28,800
- Guys, I'm trying to
figure out a way to get us

601
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:30,469
all out of here, okay?

602
00:24:30,469 --> 00:24:33,505
- So, so like if you find a
big box marked "Hamdingers",

603
00:24:33,505 --> 00:24:35,040
you're not gonna?

604
00:24:35,040 --> 00:24:36,274
- Guys, there are no more
boxes marked "Hamdingers".

605
00:24:36,274 --> 00:24:37,476
I know that.

606
00:24:37,476 --> 00:24:39,811
Look, I promise I
won't leave you, okay?

607
00:24:39,811 --> 00:24:42,080
Come on, cheer up. Cheese fight!

608
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:45,050
(Tom and Crow laughing)

609
00:24:45,050 --> 00:24:47,519
- Oh hey, cut it out!
- It's fun!

610
00:24:47,519 --> 00:24:48,820
- [Gypsy] Guys, guys!

611
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:50,088
- What is it, Gypsy?

612
00:24:50,088 --> 00:24:51,990
- [Gypsy] It's not cheese.

613
00:24:51,990 --> 00:24:54,593
(all groaning)

614
00:24:54,593 --> 00:24:57,195
(upbeat music)

615
00:25:26,291 --> 00:25:27,492
- [Mike] If it's not
cheese, what is it?

616
00:25:27,492 --> 00:25:28,793
- [Tom] It tasted like cheese.

617
00:25:28,793 --> 00:25:29,628
- I like it.

618
00:25:30,795 --> 00:25:32,531
- [Bill] For God's
sake, open the door!

619
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:34,499
- [Mike] Hey, it's
Johnny Tremaine.

620
00:25:34,499 --> 00:25:36,268
- Keep your head on! I'm coming!

621
00:25:37,602 --> 00:25:40,338
- [Tom] You won't
believe the day I've had.

622
00:25:40,338 --> 00:25:41,640
- A terrible accident.

623
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:43,942
I've got to save her,
I've got to save her.

624
00:25:43,942 --> 00:25:45,343
- What is it? What
have you got there?

625
00:25:45,343 --> 00:25:46,811
- Kurt, please.
- It's mine.

626
00:25:46,811 --> 00:25:48,380
- We'll have to tune some
instruments, quickly.

627
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:49,915
- What are you going to do?

628
00:25:49,915 --> 00:25:51,082
Aren't you going to have a
look in the closet first?

629
00:25:51,082 --> 00:25:52,751
- No, I can't now.
This is more important.

630
00:25:52,751 --> 00:25:53,952
- But you don't understand.

631
00:25:53,952 --> 00:25:55,120
- For God's sake,
Kurt! This is urgent.

632
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:56,388
Do as I tell you
before it's too late.

633
00:25:56,388 --> 00:25:57,956
I can't waste precious
time arguing with you.

634
00:25:57,956 --> 00:26:00,325
- [Mike] How was my day?
Oh, it's not important.

635
00:26:01,960 --> 00:26:05,030
- No time to mourn.
Got to get to work.

636
00:26:05,030 --> 00:26:07,866
(suspenseful music)

637
00:26:07,866 --> 00:26:09,601
- [Mike] Okay. And pencils down.

638
00:26:09,601 --> 00:26:11,102
How much did you risk? Ooh.

639
00:26:12,571 --> 00:26:13,872
- [Tom] This'll
get my pinot noir

640
00:26:13,872 --> 00:26:15,807
that Tawny knows I've
been looking for.

641
00:26:20,145 --> 00:26:21,846
- [Mike] So he's
a scientist, then.

642
00:26:21,846 --> 00:26:22,847
- [Tom] Pretty much.

643
00:26:25,584 --> 00:26:29,454
- Is it honey he's putting
in the KC Masterpiece sauce?

644
00:26:31,656 --> 00:26:34,459
- Dude, he's wiring
his lab for KWAN.

645
00:26:34,459 --> 00:26:35,794
- Pro Logic.
- Huh.

646
00:26:39,731 --> 00:26:42,033
- [Mike] Man. I am tearing
through the Brawny.

647
00:26:45,537 --> 00:26:46,438
- Should I?

648
00:26:52,310 --> 00:26:53,678
- [Tom] One, two, three.

649
00:26:53,678 --> 00:26:56,014
Aw, cripes, that
looks about right.

650
00:26:56,014 --> 00:26:58,416
As long as I'm close.
This isn't rocket science.

651
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:03,722
- [Mike] I wonder how
dad's doing in Denver.

652
00:27:03,722 --> 00:27:04,990
Hope his flight was okay.

653
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:10,996
- [Tom] I bet he's going to
turn her into Mrs. Olson.

654
00:27:10,996 --> 00:27:13,498
(Tom humming)

655
00:27:15,734 --> 00:27:20,205
- That flask has an
hourglass figure. It's hot.

656
00:27:20,205 --> 00:27:21,039
Oh baby.

657
00:27:23,575 --> 00:27:24,843
- [Mike] Well, his
methods are unusual,

658
00:27:24,843 --> 00:27:26,244
but his coffee's great.

659
00:27:29,147 --> 00:27:31,650
- [Tom] Well, a little of this
couldn't hurt, what the hell.

660
00:27:33,084 --> 00:27:36,755
- Oh, you put the lime in the
coconut and drink it all up!

661
00:27:36,755 --> 00:27:39,658
- [Mike] Say,
Doctor. It's Darkman.

662
00:27:41,926 --> 00:27:44,462
- [Tom] Done. Now time
to make my own Bailey's.

663
00:27:45,930 --> 00:27:47,932
- With a Matthews clock, you
always know what time it is.

664
00:27:47,932 --> 00:27:49,034
Now back to our-

665
00:27:52,203 --> 00:27:54,105
- [Mike] This is the most
complicated beer bong

666
00:27:54,105 --> 00:27:55,173
I've ever seen.

667
00:27:56,708 --> 00:27:58,009
(Tom humming)

668
00:27:58,009 --> 00:27:59,744
"An American in Vitro".

669
00:28:03,882 --> 00:28:06,184
- They saved Sister
Bertrille's brain.

670
00:28:08,153 --> 00:28:10,655
- [Tom] Boss, you've broken
the goofy meter again.

671
00:28:11,923 --> 00:28:15,260
- I saw them move.

672
00:28:15,260 --> 00:28:16,194
It can't be.

673
00:28:16,194 --> 00:28:17,128
- [Tom] She won't be able to do

674
00:28:17,128 --> 00:28:18,897
any heavy lifting for a while.

675
00:28:18,897 --> 00:28:20,665
- My eyes are deceiving me.

676
00:28:22,067 --> 00:28:23,268
- What you see is real.

677
00:28:23,268 --> 00:28:24,836
- [Mike] What you
smell is unfortunate.

678
00:28:24,836 --> 00:28:27,072
- What's done is done and
what I've done is right.

679
00:28:28,306 --> 00:28:30,175
It's the work of science.

680
00:28:30,175 --> 00:28:32,010
- Science? I thought
you said pizza.

681
00:28:35,346 --> 00:28:37,982
(Jan whimpering)

682
00:28:37,982 --> 00:28:40,051
- [Tom] Could you
scratch my nose, honey?

683
00:28:41,653 --> 00:28:43,154
(Jan whimpering)

684
00:28:43,154 --> 00:28:44,556
She talks like Clutch Cargo.

685
00:28:48,426 --> 00:28:50,261
- Doesn't she need lungs?

686
00:28:50,261 --> 00:28:51,629
[Tom] No, she's got neck juice.

687
00:28:51,629 --> 00:28:52,464
- Oh.

688
00:28:55,200 --> 00:28:56,301
- Let me die.

689
00:28:57,469 --> 00:28:58,970
- [Tom] I'm sorry?

690
00:29:00,171 --> 00:29:01,372
- Let me die.

691
00:29:01,372 --> 00:29:02,607
- [Mike] Oh, you're
welcome, honey.

692
00:29:04,042 --> 00:29:05,643
- I've had success
with transplants.

693
00:29:05,643 --> 00:29:07,011
Now I can do it for her.

694
00:29:07,011 --> 00:29:08,613
- You can change
your hair color,

695
00:29:08,613 --> 00:29:10,081
give her a complete makeover.

696
00:29:11,783 --> 00:29:13,752
- Transplant her to what?

697
00:29:13,752 --> 00:29:14,886
- Larry Storch.

698
00:29:14,886 --> 00:29:16,254
- I'll bring her back.

699
00:29:16,254 --> 00:29:18,990
She'll live and I'll
get her another body.

700
00:29:18,990 --> 00:29:20,625
I can make her complete again.

701
00:29:20,625 --> 00:29:22,694
- [Tom] A little shorter
maybe, but still.

702
00:29:22,694 --> 00:29:24,429
- Only a madman can believe

703
00:29:24,429 --> 00:29:26,464
that she could ever
be like before.

704
00:29:26,464 --> 00:29:27,565
- Don't argue with me.

705
00:29:28,533 --> 00:29:29,467
- Okay.
- I love her too much

706
00:29:29,467 --> 00:29:30,902
to let her stay like this.

707
00:29:31,936 --> 00:29:33,938
I'll restore her as before.

708
00:29:33,938 --> 00:29:34,773
You'll see.

709
00:29:36,274 --> 00:29:38,810
- Can't you realize?
Can't you see?

710
00:29:38,810 --> 00:29:41,279
There's a pattern
to all that lives.

711
00:29:41,279 --> 00:29:43,848
An order, an arrangement.

712
00:29:43,848 --> 00:29:46,451
She had a heart and a brain.

713
00:29:46,451 --> 00:29:47,952
- And the nerve!

714
00:29:47,952 --> 00:29:49,154
- Her spirit was in both,
not in one or the other.

715
00:29:49,154 --> 00:29:50,822
- [Mike] Now she's
a paperweight.

716
00:29:52,023 --> 00:29:53,658
- I'll give her a
brain and a heart.

717
00:29:53,658 --> 00:29:55,460
- Yes, and what of her soul?

718
00:29:55,460 --> 00:29:56,461
- It's in the car.

719
00:29:57,929 --> 00:29:59,664
- You say you love her,

720
00:29:59,664 --> 00:30:01,733
and you can remember
her love for you.

721
00:30:01,733 --> 00:30:03,935
- [Tom] As you can see folks,
no strings, no mirrors.

722
00:30:03,935 --> 00:30:05,837
- Yet you've made of her
an experiment of horror.

723
00:30:05,837 --> 00:30:08,139
- [Mike] Shut up, man.
You're lucky my chick's here.

724
00:30:08,139 --> 00:30:10,875
- All the skill in science I
possess was meant for this.

725
00:30:10,875 --> 00:30:12,477
Life has a pattern.

726
00:30:13,812 --> 00:30:15,613
The whole pattern of my
life is shaping itself

727
00:30:15,613 --> 00:30:16,548
to save her now.

728
00:30:18,016 --> 00:30:19,551
- Then you intend to
go through with it?

729
00:30:19,551 --> 00:30:20,785
- Yes.

730
00:30:20,785 --> 00:30:21,953
- Well, at least you don't have

731
00:30:21,953 --> 00:30:24,489
delusions of
grandeur or anything.

732
00:30:24,489 --> 00:30:25,490
- Sleep, my darling.

733
00:30:27,325 --> 00:30:28,693
Rest and grow strong.

734
00:30:28,693 --> 00:30:30,528
- [Tom] Can I get
a neck pillow, hon?

735
00:30:33,464 --> 00:30:34,632
- [Mike] Okay, now quiet, Kurt.

736
00:30:34,632 --> 00:30:36,301
I just put her
down for the night.

737
00:30:39,070 --> 00:30:41,840
- Come here. I've got a great
idea for a practical joke.

738
00:30:42,907 --> 00:30:44,175
- [Mike] Put her neck in water.

739
00:30:44,175 --> 00:30:45,009
- How long do you think
we can keep her alive

740
00:30:45,009 --> 00:30:46,277
under these conditions?

741
00:30:46,277 --> 00:30:47,612
- [Mike] Til she gets
pan sores, I guess.

742
00:30:48,780 --> 00:30:50,148
- 50 Hours at the most.

743
00:30:52,383 --> 00:30:56,454
- Do you really believe you
can work a transplant on her?

744
00:30:56,454 --> 00:30:57,755
- [Tom] Nope.

745
00:30:57,755 --> 00:30:58,723
- Successfully?

746
00:30:58,723 --> 00:30:59,891
- Yes.
- Uh, no.

747
00:31:02,227 --> 00:31:03,394
- Like my arm.

748
00:31:04,529 --> 00:31:05,997
Withered and deformed.

749
00:31:05,997 --> 00:31:07,465
- [Tom] His left
hand's pretty bad too.

750
00:31:07,465 --> 00:31:09,667
- Yours was an early
experiment that failed.

751
00:31:10,869 --> 00:31:12,737
With her, I'm using
my new adreno serum.

752
00:31:12,737 --> 00:31:13,571
- Oh, the neck juice.

753
00:31:13,571 --> 00:31:14,873
- It must work.

754
00:31:14,873 --> 00:31:17,041
- [Mike] It's got no
fat and half the salt.

755
00:31:18,376 --> 00:31:19,444
- I've got to go now.

756
00:31:20,445 --> 00:31:24,015
If the police or anyone call.

757
00:31:24,015 --> 00:31:25,750
- [Tom] Have her get it.

758
00:31:25,750 --> 00:31:28,119
- Tell them you don't
know anything about it.

759
00:31:28,119 --> 00:31:29,587
- [Mike] Honey.

760
00:31:29,587 --> 00:31:30,788
- I don't think anyone
will trace us here because

761
00:31:30,788 --> 00:31:32,156
her body was burned
in the wreckage.

762
00:31:33,057 --> 00:31:34,492
- Yes, of course, of course.

763
00:31:34,492 --> 00:31:35,727
Look, Bill, before you go.

764
00:31:35,727 --> 00:31:37,195
- Could you give
me a sponge bath?

765
00:31:37,195 --> 00:31:38,429
- Could you have a
look in that closet?

766
00:31:38,429 --> 00:31:39,898
It's the reason why
I called you up here.

767
00:31:39,898 --> 00:31:42,400
- [Tom] Oh, so that's why you
had me kill my girlfriend.

768
00:31:42,400 --> 00:31:44,135
So I could see in the closet?

769
00:31:44,135 --> 00:31:45,536
- [Crow] It looks
really nice in there.

770
00:31:45,536 --> 00:31:47,338
I got a new organizer
and everything.

771
00:31:50,508 --> 00:31:52,243
- [Mike] God speed, my friend.

772
00:31:53,678 --> 00:31:55,513
- Okay, here he comes,
here he comes. Shh.

773
00:31:55,513 --> 00:31:58,883
You need to keep it
down. Okay, shh, shh.

774
00:32:01,152 --> 00:32:03,688
- [Tom] You knuckle-knob.
What do you have in here?

775
00:32:05,723 --> 00:32:07,759
- Sounds like crazy
Guggenheims in there.

776
00:32:11,829 --> 00:32:13,431
- [Tom] Hello, hello?

777
00:32:13,431 --> 00:32:15,333
Looks like the Soupy Sales show.

778
00:32:16,567 --> 00:32:17,669
- Hello, hello, hello, hello.

779
00:32:19,904 --> 00:32:23,474
(creature growling)

780
00:32:23,474 --> 00:32:26,077
- [Mike] Ew, Dylan Thomas'
last moments on Earth.

781
00:32:30,982 --> 00:32:31,916
- Keep it locked.

782
00:32:31,916 --> 00:32:33,785
- And keep the litter box clean.

783
00:32:33,785 --> 00:32:35,286
- It almost broke out.

784
00:32:37,055 --> 00:32:38,690
- Don't worry about it.
Just keep it closed.

785
00:32:38,690 --> 00:32:39,891
- [Tom] I thought you'd love it.

786
00:32:39,891 --> 00:32:41,225
Didn't just see the
hat I put on it?

787
00:32:43,628 --> 00:32:44,896
- [Mike] Well, thank
you for showing me that.

788
00:32:44,896 --> 00:32:47,298
It made my day a
little bit brighter.

789
00:32:50,601 --> 00:32:52,170
- I have to find her a body.

790
00:32:52,170 --> 00:32:54,238
- How are you going to
go about getting one?

791
00:32:56,240 --> 00:32:58,943
- I'm not even gonna
dignify that with an answer.

792
00:32:58,943 --> 00:33:00,244
- Bill, how will you do it?

793
00:33:01,679 --> 00:33:03,247
- There are ways.

794
00:33:03,247 --> 00:33:06,351
(Tom laughing maniacally)

795
00:33:06,351 --> 00:33:07,352
There are ways.

796
00:33:07,352 --> 00:33:09,854
- [Mike] Lots of
ways, many ways.

797
00:33:09,854 --> 00:33:11,222
Have I mentioned there are ways?

798
00:33:13,224 --> 00:33:14,092
- [Tom] Ways.

799
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:18,429
(upbeat music)

800
00:33:46,958 --> 00:33:49,227
- [Mike] Hey, he walked
all the way to Paris.

801
00:33:50,595 --> 00:33:52,697
(sensual music)

802
00:33:52,697 --> 00:33:55,566
- [Mike] Say, this may
actually be an upgrade.

803
00:33:58,202 --> 00:33:59,804
- [Tom] She can't
have a cardboard body.

804
00:33:59,804 --> 00:34:00,805
I've ruled that out.

805
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:05,576
- [Mike] My name is Dirk,
what's yours? (coughing)

806
00:34:09,847 --> 00:34:14,185
- Ooh, this is the one big
silver lining of my day.

807
00:34:14,185 --> 00:34:16,921
- [Tom] It's nice and all,
but I want something sleazy.

808
00:34:21,926 --> 00:34:24,095
- [Mike] If Jack
Ruby owned a Denny's.

809
00:34:26,197 --> 00:34:27,932
- I hope that's not Rose Marie.

810
00:34:30,201 --> 00:34:31,836
- [Mike] Hey look, the
panel from "What's My Line?"

811
00:34:31,836 --> 00:34:32,937
is behind her.

812
00:34:34,906 --> 00:34:37,675
- [Tom] Pasties and a
g-string, beer and a sharp,

813
00:34:37,675 --> 00:34:40,745
boil 'em through a shot glass
and buffalo squeeze, ha ha.

814
00:34:45,249 --> 00:34:46,684
- He's helping me. I'm fine.

815
00:34:48,119 --> 00:34:50,822
- [Tom] Our soup of the day
is cauliflower au gratin.

816
00:34:50,822 --> 00:34:54,325
Today's special is Salisbury
steak and German potato salad.

817
00:34:54,325 --> 00:34:56,661
- [Mike] Oh, well, I'll
just see a menu, thank you.

818
00:34:56,661 --> 00:34:58,296
- I am the menu.

819
00:35:01,966 --> 00:35:05,036
- [Tom] I'm just curious.
What size collar do you wear?

820
00:35:10,741 --> 00:35:13,344
- Senor Wences and
Evita, out on the town.

821
00:35:14,579 --> 00:35:15,513
Talk to your hand.

822
00:35:16,848 --> 00:35:18,850
- [Tom] Welcome to
the Diane Arbus Cafe.

823
00:35:21,886 --> 00:35:24,689
- A night on the town
in Escanaba, Michigan.

824
00:35:26,324 --> 00:35:28,292
- [Mike] This dance
represents that there

825
00:35:28,292 --> 00:35:31,162
are no baked potatoes
til after five.

826
00:35:33,564 --> 00:35:35,733
- [Tom] Jojo was a man who
thought he was a woman.

827
00:35:37,568 --> 00:35:40,138
(Tom clucking)

828
00:35:44,242 --> 00:35:47,512
- The spokesmodel competition.

829
00:35:47,512 --> 00:35:48,813
- Rocky Graziano!

830
00:35:51,916 --> 00:35:54,519
- [Mike] I must have blood
before the night is done.

831
00:35:58,756 --> 00:36:00,458
- [Tom] Hey gang, there's
a snuff film playing

832
00:36:00,458 --> 00:36:01,859
over at the Rialto, let's go!

833
00:36:01,859 --> 00:36:04,462
(all cheering)

834
00:36:06,130 --> 00:36:09,800
- Oh, wait for me.
(laughing awkwardly)

835
00:36:12,870 --> 00:36:15,306
- [Mike] Mr. Graziano,
can I have your autograph?

836
00:36:17,742 --> 00:36:22,013
♪ I'm getting
sentimental over you ♪

837
00:36:23,080 --> 00:36:24,315
- Boy, what a shift.

838
00:36:28,486 --> 00:36:29,754
- [Mike] He's rocking.

839
00:36:29,754 --> 00:36:30,955
Who would've thought
a few hours earlier

840
00:36:30,955 --> 00:36:32,990
he was cradling his
wife's head in his lap.

841
00:36:34,759 --> 00:36:37,061
- [Tom] The 1958 floozy.

842
00:36:38,896 --> 00:36:41,666
(Crow squeaking)

843
00:36:43,935 --> 00:36:46,771
- Oh, hi. Have you
seen my chests?

844
00:36:48,272 --> 00:36:51,576
So, you a goer, eh? Goer,
nudge nudge, know what I mean?

845
00:36:58,182 --> 00:37:00,384
- Meanwhile, at
Kurt Cobain's house.

846
00:37:00,384 --> 00:37:01,852
Oh.

847
00:37:01,852 --> 00:37:03,988
- [Mike] Dave is such a
dink for not letting me die.

848
00:37:08,492 --> 00:37:10,595
- He's keeping her
alive with Grey Poupon.

849
00:37:14,832 --> 00:37:17,902
- [Tom] Boy, that was
an important scene, huh?

850
00:37:17,902 --> 00:37:19,036
- [Mike] So you're
you're saying you're

851
00:37:19,036 --> 00:37:20,338
not into dismemberment?

852
00:37:26,277 --> 00:37:29,180
- Well, back into
the broom closet.

853
00:37:33,084 --> 00:37:34,619
- Here's Abe Burrows
and Jimmy Hoffa

854
00:37:34,619 --> 00:37:36,187
entertaining Eleanor Roosevelt.

855
00:37:40,791 --> 00:37:42,460
- [Mike] Don't
wait up, everyone.

856
00:37:46,564 --> 00:37:49,400
- [Tom] Ma'am, may I pour you
a buttock, a nip, a drink?

857
00:37:50,601 --> 00:37:52,670
- What took you so long?

858
00:37:52,670 --> 00:37:54,472
- What made you think I'd come?

859
00:37:54,472 --> 00:37:56,941
- You know a good
thing when you see it.

860
00:37:56,941 --> 00:37:58,109
- Yeah, there was plenty to see.

861
00:37:58,109 --> 00:37:59,343
- And the Skillet
Scrambler was numtious.

862
00:37:59,343 --> 00:38:00,845
- I liked your act.

863
00:38:00,845 --> 00:38:02,313
- Is that all you liked?

864
00:38:02,313 --> 00:38:05,816
- Well, your costume, what there
was of it, was interesting.

865
00:38:05,816 --> 00:38:07,285
- Is that all?

866
00:38:07,285 --> 00:38:08,486
- [Tom] Well, I liked
your Nicholson impression.

867
00:38:08,486 --> 00:38:11,389
- The rest of the
equipment is standard.

868
00:38:11,389 --> 00:38:14,625
But, uh, the arrangement
is pretty special.

869
00:38:14,625 --> 00:38:17,128
- [Mike] Oh, by Elia Kazan.

870
00:38:17,128 --> 00:38:19,997
- You're no tourist just
looking at the sights.

871
00:38:19,997 --> 00:38:22,466
What are you doing
here? Slumming?

872
00:38:22,466 --> 00:38:24,302
- Well, I get my
allowance once a week.

873
00:38:24,302 --> 00:38:27,772
- Okay, if you're so loaded,
you pay for the drink.

874
00:38:27,772 --> 00:38:28,773
- I already did.

875
00:38:28,773 --> 00:38:29,974
- Oh, I ate your
shift meal, too.

876
00:38:29,974 --> 00:38:31,609
- Are you hustling
for the house?

877
00:38:31,609 --> 00:38:32,943
- I hustle for myself.

878
00:38:32,943 --> 00:38:34,211
- [Mike] Oh, sorry
I offended you.

879
00:38:34,211 --> 00:38:35,846
You really have a
lot of integrity.

880
00:38:37,048 --> 00:38:39,183
- I'm the leading
lady around here.

881
00:38:39,183 --> 00:38:41,886
I can dance for the
squares out front,

882
00:38:41,886 --> 00:38:44,922
or I can relax back
here with my friends.

883
00:38:46,324 --> 00:38:48,359
- I'll bet you don't have
an enemy in the world.

884
00:38:48,359 --> 00:38:51,429
- [Tom] Well, I had a
falling out with Delta Burke.

885
00:38:51,429 --> 00:38:52,863
Who hasn't?

886
00:38:52,863 --> 00:38:54,598
- Hi, I'm Casey Kasem.

887
00:38:54,598 --> 00:38:58,002
This one goes out to a heartsick
lover with a severed head.

888
00:38:59,203 --> 00:39:00,671
- [Mike] Finally,
I'm a size eight.

889
00:39:04,842 --> 00:39:06,043
- Let me die.

890
00:39:06,043 --> 00:39:07,545
- [Tom] Are you
still on that kick?

891
00:39:07,545 --> 00:39:10,014
Come on, pull yourself up by
the collar tabs and get a job.

892
00:39:13,918 --> 00:39:15,019
- Let me die.

893
00:39:16,153 --> 00:39:17,621
- After you've
cleaned your room.

894
00:39:18,989 --> 00:39:21,559
- [Mike] To recap, let me die.

895
00:39:21,559 --> 00:39:23,294
- Where are you from?

896
00:39:23,294 --> 00:39:24,362
- Oh, around.

897
00:39:24,362 --> 00:39:26,230
Just looking around.

898
00:39:26,230 --> 00:39:29,467
- When you get done
looking, then what?

899
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:31,635
- I operate.

900
00:39:31,635 --> 00:39:34,538
- I get your message.
It's coming across.

901
00:39:34,538 --> 00:39:36,240
- [Tom] So, uh, what do you do?

902
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:39,377
- You could flip any chick
in the house. Why me?

903
00:39:39,377 --> 00:39:40,578
- [Mike] Why not scramble me?

904
00:39:40,578 --> 00:39:42,446
- Well, like I
said, I'm looking.

905
00:39:42,446 --> 00:39:45,182
- And you don't have
to look any further.

906
00:39:45,182 --> 00:39:46,817
I'm not gonna fake it for you.

907
00:39:47,985 --> 00:39:50,421
- When do you go on
again, huh? When?

908
00:39:50,421 --> 00:39:51,789
- Relax.

909
00:39:51,789 --> 00:39:54,558
I got another show yet,
but it could be for you.

910
00:39:55,559 --> 00:39:56,627
- I was just asking.

911
00:39:57,962 --> 00:39:59,196
- I don't feel good.

912
00:39:59,196 --> 00:40:03,601
- You're shaking. Are
you getting nervous?

913
00:40:03,601 --> 00:40:05,136
I'm over 21.

914
00:40:05,136 --> 00:40:06,704
- [Tom] 21? She's over 41.

915
00:40:06,704 --> 00:40:08,005
- Oh, it isn't that, it's...

916
00:40:08,005 --> 00:40:09,206
I just got things to do.

917
00:40:09,206 --> 00:40:11,375
Things to take care of.

918
00:40:12,543 --> 00:40:13,944
- Well, if doing that
will make you sick,

919
00:40:13,944 --> 00:40:15,179
what could be more important

920
00:40:15,179 --> 00:40:17,448
than to be taken
care of right now?

921
00:40:17,448 --> 00:40:18,783
- Are you coming on to me?

922
00:40:20,184 --> 00:40:23,387
- But I've, I've got to
see about helping somebody,

923
00:40:25,055 --> 00:40:26,390
somebody who needs
my help very badly.

924
00:40:26,390 --> 00:40:28,325
- [Mike] Hey, this,
this is sick. Ugh.

925
00:40:29,493 --> 00:40:31,295
- Not when you've got
me feeling so good.

926
00:40:32,329 --> 00:40:33,931
I'm so warm all over.

927
00:40:35,332 --> 00:40:39,370
I know I'm good for you, I know
I'm good for what you want.

928
00:40:39,370 --> 00:40:41,005
- [Tom] They are talking
about sex, aren't they?

929
00:40:41,005 --> 00:40:42,540
- Really?
- I thought so.

930
00:40:42,540 --> 00:40:44,308
- You may be just
what I'm looking for.

931
00:40:46,243 --> 00:40:48,612
- [Mike] Ew.
(Crow whimpering)

932
00:40:48,612 --> 00:40:51,148
- Lucy Arnaz is back
and she's pissed.

933
00:40:51,148 --> 00:40:53,083
- You've got your nerve.

934
00:40:53,083 --> 00:40:55,119
- Oh, look who's talking.

935
00:40:56,320 --> 00:40:57,588
- Why don't you haul
your beat-up body

936
00:40:57,588 --> 00:40:59,824
back to the bar with
the rest of the flies?

937
00:40:59,824 --> 00:41:01,325
- Keep your g-string on.

938
00:41:01,325 --> 00:41:03,928
I only came in here
to change my clothes.

939
00:41:03,928 --> 00:41:05,563
(all shouting)

940
00:41:05,563 --> 00:41:08,199
I'd like to see the
rest of the show.

941
00:41:08,199 --> 00:41:11,202
- Come back in half an
hour and maybe you will.

942
00:41:11,202 --> 00:41:12,436
- Get lost.

943
00:41:12,436 --> 00:41:13,704
- [Tom] Could you
get rid of your dad?

944
00:41:13,704 --> 00:41:15,539
- Hi, lover boy.

945
00:41:15,539 --> 00:41:16,373
- Same to you.

946
00:41:19,243 --> 00:41:22,913
- Hey, come here. Don't hide.

947
00:41:22,913 --> 00:41:24,748
You know, you've
got the kind of face

948
00:41:24,748 --> 00:41:26,517
a girl doesn't mind looking at.

949
00:41:26,517 --> 00:41:29,386
Even up front, all the other
girls were asking about you.

950
00:41:29,386 --> 00:41:30,888
- [Mike] Really?
What'd they say?

951
00:41:30,888 --> 00:41:33,357
- Get out of here. Two's
company, three's a crowd.

952
00:41:33,357 --> 00:41:34,592
- [Tom] Zing.

953
00:41:34,592 --> 00:41:36,494
- Who's to tell me to
blow if I don't want to?

954
00:41:36,494 --> 00:41:40,297
This here's my dressing
room too, remember.

955
00:41:40,297 --> 00:41:42,600
- It kills her to
see me make time.

956
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:44,768
- You're the only thing
that's going to be made

957
00:41:44,768 --> 00:41:46,804
around here tonight, honey.

958
00:41:46,804 --> 00:41:48,572
- Eat your heart out.

959
00:41:48,572 --> 00:41:49,540
- [Tom] Well.

960
00:41:49,540 --> 00:41:51,642
- Well, I'd better be going.

961
00:41:51,642 --> 00:41:53,410
- It's been sleazy
and all, but, uh.

962
00:41:53,410 --> 00:41:55,246
- What for?
- Look what you've done.

963
00:41:55,246 --> 00:41:56,313
- Oh, no, no.

964
00:41:57,248 --> 00:41:58,482
- No no no no no, stop.

965
00:41:58,482 --> 00:42:00,684
- Hey, desist, don't you!

966
00:42:00,684 --> 00:42:02,419
Don't you.
- Freeze.

967
00:42:02,419 --> 00:42:03,687
- Oh no!

968
00:42:03,687 --> 00:42:05,756
(all shouting)

969
00:42:05,756 --> 00:42:06,991
- I'll remember you.

970
00:42:08,092 --> 00:42:09,627
- Yeah, that's
what I'm afraid of.

971
00:42:14,431 --> 00:42:16,267
- [Mike] Do you
think he likes me?

972
00:42:16,267 --> 00:42:17,701
- You lousy tramp.

973
00:42:17,701 --> 00:42:20,371
Once in a blue moon I latch
onto a man with class,

974
00:42:20,371 --> 00:42:22,473
and you messed it up.

975
00:42:22,473 --> 00:42:23,774
- [Mike] What?

976
00:42:23,774 --> 00:42:26,410
- What makes him
think you had him?

977
00:42:26,410 --> 00:42:28,078
He wouldn't have you on a bet.

978
00:42:28,078 --> 00:42:29,680
- Says who?

979
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:30,981
- Says me.

980
00:42:30,981 --> 00:42:33,918
What's a guy like that
want leftovers for?

981
00:42:33,918 --> 00:42:34,985
- Leftovers?

982
00:42:36,020 --> 00:42:37,121
- Shannon Doherty, no!

983
00:42:38,556 --> 00:42:41,225
- [Tom] Yes. Danny Bonaduce
in the fight of his life.

984
00:42:42,393 --> 00:42:43,761
This is grotesque. I
gotta get out of here.

985
00:42:43,761 --> 00:42:45,029
Take us out of this.

986
00:42:45,029 --> 00:42:46,697
- No, finally something
I can really get behind.

987
00:42:46,697 --> 00:42:50,100
Why don't you guys,
okay, I'm outta here.

988
00:42:53,671 --> 00:42:55,639
(cat meowing)

989
00:42:55,639 --> 00:42:58,175
(upbeat music)

990
00:43:26,337 --> 00:43:27,504
- Well, new feller.

991
00:43:27,504 --> 00:43:28,739
Gotta hand it to you.

992
00:43:28,739 --> 00:43:31,008
You got us going on
a project right away,

993
00:43:31,008 --> 00:43:34,278
having us design hats for the
lady in the movie, why you.

994
00:43:35,212 --> 00:43:36,714
- I just feel so sorry for her.

995
00:43:36,714 --> 00:43:39,049
Everything she must
be going through.

996
00:43:39,049 --> 00:43:42,886
Her fiance is mental, and
she's lost her independence.

997
00:43:42,886 --> 00:43:45,623
- Not to mention trying to
minimize your major figure flaw.

998
00:43:45,623 --> 00:43:47,057
- Yeah.

999
00:43:47,057 --> 00:43:48,325
- Well, I've always
thought that the right hat

1000
00:43:48,325 --> 00:43:49,827
can make you look
and feel terrific.

1001
00:43:49,827 --> 00:43:52,796
And that's why for her, I
designed a bowling bag hat.

1002
00:43:52,796 --> 00:43:54,198
She can zip it around herself

1003
00:43:54,198 --> 00:43:55,733
and just be toted anywhere.

1004
00:43:55,733 --> 00:43:57,034
- That's really clever there.

1005
00:43:57,034 --> 00:43:59,637
You know, my idea
was simply a crown

1006
00:43:59,637 --> 00:44:02,539
for that regal Katharine
Hepburn "Lion in Winter" look.

1007
00:44:04,074 --> 00:44:05,576
- Personally, I can't wear hats,

1008
00:44:05,576 --> 00:44:07,678
but I thought she'd looked
neat in my chianti hat.

1009
00:44:07,678 --> 00:44:09,780
- Oh, that is neat. Yeah.

1010
00:44:09,780 --> 00:44:11,081
- Very nice, very nice.

1011
00:44:11,081 --> 00:44:12,549
- You know, as a fellow woman,

1012
00:44:12,549 --> 00:44:15,285
I knew she'd want to create
the illusion of height.

1013
00:44:15,285 --> 00:44:18,522
So I came up with
this crown roast hat.

1014
00:44:18,522 --> 00:44:22,026
Which also serves six.

1015
00:44:22,026 --> 00:44:25,863
With a side of snow
peas and new potatoes.

1016
00:44:25,863 --> 00:44:27,231
- Well, I hope
you like this one.

1017
00:44:27,231 --> 00:44:28,499
I worked really hard on it.

1018
00:44:28,499 --> 00:44:30,401
To me, it sounded really cool

1019
00:44:30,401 --> 00:44:32,169
to give her a kinetic ball hat.

1020
00:44:33,203 --> 00:44:34,672
A real conversation starter.

1021
00:44:34,672 --> 00:44:37,808
- Put it on the
desk, aw. It works.

1022
00:44:37,808 --> 00:44:41,178
- This raspberry beret
just screamed fun to me.

1023
00:44:41,178 --> 00:44:43,847
- Well, let me just say
that form follows function

1024
00:44:43,847 --> 00:44:45,916
in my lazy Susan hat.

1025
00:44:45,916 --> 00:44:48,786
This way, she's really
the center of attention

1026
00:44:48,786 --> 00:44:50,387
at any dinner function.

1027
00:44:50,387 --> 00:44:52,956
(all laughing)

1028
00:44:56,126 --> 00:44:58,128
- Well? Fun, huh?

1029
00:44:58,128 --> 00:44:59,596
- Yeah, fun.

1030
00:44:59,596 --> 00:45:01,065
- I don't know.

1031
00:45:01,065 --> 00:45:03,333
I just have this feeling
she's really a shoes person.

1032
00:45:04,835 --> 00:45:05,669
- Huh.

1033
00:45:05,669 --> 00:45:07,771
(alarm blaring)

1034
00:45:07,771 --> 00:45:10,374
(all shouting)

1035
00:45:21,085 --> 00:45:22,720
- [Mike] Well, that
was my project, anyway.

1036
00:45:22,720 --> 00:45:24,655
- No, it was very good.
- Thank you.

1037
00:45:24,655 --> 00:45:25,723
- It was fun.

1038
00:45:26,957 --> 00:45:28,459
- [Mike] Oh, yeah.

1039
00:45:28,459 --> 00:45:30,060
- [Jan] What has he done to you?

1040
00:45:30,060 --> 00:45:32,896
- [Tom] What hasn't
he done to me?

1041
00:45:32,896 --> 00:45:35,399
(eerie music)

1042
00:45:36,500 --> 00:45:39,069
- I know there's someone there.

1043
00:45:40,537 --> 00:45:42,706
- She's trying to out someone
that doesn't want to be outed.

1044
00:45:44,441 --> 00:45:46,310
- Knock once if you hear me.

1045
00:45:46,310 --> 00:45:49,813
- [Mike] Knock three times on
the ceiling if you want me.

1046
00:45:49,813 --> 00:45:52,616
- [Tom] Oh good,
they're making friends.

1047
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:58,355
- [Mike] Battery acid?
You're soaking in it.

1048
00:45:59,289 --> 00:46:00,524
- [Tom] It's like a pork hock.

1049
00:46:00,524 --> 00:46:02,092
- Jan in the Pan,
are you still here?

1050
00:46:03,961 --> 00:46:05,562
- [Tom] Aw, doggone it.

1051
00:46:05,562 --> 00:46:07,798
I always set down my
coffee and then lose it.

1052
00:46:09,433 --> 00:46:10,434
It's the rabbit mug.

1053
00:46:11,969 --> 00:46:13,370
- And I'm not the first.

1054
00:46:15,205 --> 00:46:18,008
Knock twice if
I'm not the first.

1055
00:46:18,008 --> 00:46:20,711
(knocking twice)

1056
00:46:20,711 --> 00:46:22,212
- [Mike] Oh, this is hot. Hot.

1057
00:46:26,917 --> 00:46:28,552
- He should've let me die.

1058
00:46:28,552 --> 00:46:30,521
- Oh, are you still on that?

1059
00:46:30,521 --> 00:46:32,256
Well, we've got
problems too, lady.

1060
00:46:32,256 --> 00:46:33,891
- I hate him for
what he's done to me.

1061
00:46:33,891 --> 00:46:34,792
- [Mike] Which is?

1062
00:46:36,894 --> 00:46:39,496
- If he only knew what
it's like being like this.

1063
00:46:39,496 --> 00:46:40,430
- [Tom] Oh, as if
you're the only one

1064
00:46:40,430 --> 00:46:41,899
who ever got their head cut off.

1065
00:46:41,899 --> 00:46:42,933
Quit whining!

1066
00:46:43,967 --> 00:46:46,370
- Do you know what it's like?

1067
00:46:47,604 --> 00:46:49,039
(something knocking)

1068
00:46:49,039 --> 00:46:51,875
- [Mike] Hey, it's
lights out down there.

1069
00:46:51,875 --> 00:46:53,110
Geez.

1070
00:46:53,110 --> 00:46:54,745
- Together we
could have revenge.

1071
00:46:54,745 --> 00:46:57,948
- Revenge, a dish
best served, oh.

1072
00:46:57,948 --> 00:46:59,416
- Want revenge?

1073
00:46:59,416 --> 00:47:01,752
- [Tom] No, I want to
sleep. Would you shut up?

1074
00:47:01,752 --> 00:47:04,822
(something knocking)

1075
00:47:08,792 --> 00:47:10,394
- [Mike] I want to join
in their conversation,

1076
00:47:10,394 --> 00:47:11,962
but I don't know what to say.

1077
00:47:14,364 --> 00:47:16,400
- I wonder if "Silk
Stalkings" is on yet?

1078
00:47:19,503 --> 00:47:21,705
- Yes.

1079
00:47:21,705 --> 00:47:24,541
You the thing inside, and
me the thing out here.

1080
00:47:24,541 --> 00:47:26,910
- [Tom] I'm just
freeforming here.

1081
00:47:26,910 --> 00:47:28,879
- My brain's still untouched.

1082
00:47:30,080 --> 00:47:31,615
And his keeping me
alive has given me

1083
00:47:31,615 --> 00:47:33,217
a power he didn't count on.

1084
00:47:33,217 --> 00:47:35,118
- [Mike] The power
of Matthew Star.

1085
00:47:36,420 --> 00:47:37,821
- A power that you can feel,

1086
00:47:39,456 --> 00:47:41,525
across this room and
through that door.

1087
00:47:41,525 --> 00:47:43,861
♪ To grandmother's house we go

1088
00:47:43,861 --> 00:47:45,495
- Can't you?

1089
00:47:45,495 --> 00:47:48,599
(something knocking)

1090
00:47:50,601 --> 00:47:51,635
- [Tom] Oh, I'll
tell them a joke

1091
00:47:51,635 --> 00:47:53,904
I heard on "Arsenio" last night.

1092
00:47:53,904 --> 00:47:55,906
- [Mike] Ooh, I sat
on someone's hand.

1093
00:47:55,906 --> 00:47:58,475
He leaves his experiments
lying all over the house.

1094
00:48:01,945 --> 00:48:03,113
- Get me the president.

1095
00:48:06,984 --> 00:48:08,986
- Together, we're
both more than things.

1096
00:48:10,520 --> 00:48:12,990
We have a power as hideous
as our deformities.

1097
00:48:12,990 --> 00:48:14,825
- [Tom] Speak for yourself!

1098
00:48:14,825 --> 00:48:15,926
- Together, we'll
wreak our revenge.

1099
00:48:15,926 --> 00:48:17,761
- [Mike] Well, you already reek.

1100
00:48:19,096 --> 00:48:22,566
- I shall create power.

1101
00:48:22,566 --> 00:48:24,334
And you will enforce it.

1102
00:48:24,334 --> 00:48:25,168
- That's nice, honey.

1103
00:48:25,168 --> 00:48:26,403
Look, I'm gonna turn in.

1104
00:48:26,403 --> 00:48:31,174
- You the thing inside,
me the thing out here.

1105
00:48:31,909 --> 00:48:33,410
- [Tom] Great.

1106
00:48:33,410 --> 00:48:34,411
You know, you should come
to our Tuesday night group.

1107
00:48:35,545 --> 00:48:36,780
- [Mike] I've got it.

1108
00:48:36,780 --> 00:48:38,181
If I wander around
here some more,

1109
00:48:38,181 --> 00:48:39,816
it'll keep the budget down.

1110
00:48:39,816 --> 00:48:41,985
- [Tom] Yes, yes.

1111
00:48:41,985 --> 00:48:45,088
- I'm going to make a decision.

1112
00:48:45,088 --> 00:48:46,356
No, no.

1113
00:48:46,356 --> 00:48:48,458
- [Tom] Bill Clinton
in the Oval Office.

1114
00:48:48,458 --> 00:48:49,293
- That's it!

1115
00:48:50,427 --> 00:48:51,261
No it isn't.

1116
00:48:52,963 --> 00:48:53,797
Yes! No.

1117
00:48:55,799 --> 00:48:56,733
- [Mike] Yes. No?

1118
00:48:57,668 --> 00:48:58,702
Maybe. Yes.

1119
00:48:58,702 --> 00:49:00,003
No.

1120
00:49:00,003 --> 00:49:00,804
- Yes.

1121
00:49:04,408 --> 00:49:05,542
- [Tom] Hi, you decent?

1122
00:49:08,145 --> 00:49:11,148
(suspenseful music)

1123
00:49:15,752 --> 00:49:17,187
- [Mike] I'm in the tub.

1124
00:49:17,187 --> 00:49:19,056
- She's always in the tub.

1125
00:49:19,056 --> 00:49:20,590
- [Mike] Oh.

1126
00:49:20,590 --> 00:49:21,692
- Mrs. Carmichael?

1127
00:49:26,229 --> 00:49:27,364
- [Tom] I was gonna
make some popcorn.

1128
00:49:27,364 --> 00:49:28,999
Do you want, uh, oh yeah.

1129
00:49:31,034 --> 00:49:32,469
- [Mike] Anybody seen my wallet?

1130
00:49:32,469 --> 00:49:34,338
Oh, don't get up. I'll find it.

1131
00:49:35,772 --> 00:49:37,007
- Don't mind me.

1132
00:49:37,007 --> 00:49:38,775
Just keep doing whatever
it was you were doing.

1133
00:49:43,547 --> 00:49:46,183
- Maybe she could get work
in a Peter Gabriel video.

1134
00:49:46,183 --> 00:49:47,017
- [Tom] Cool.

1135
00:49:49,820 --> 00:49:53,390
- [Mike] Hello,
Mr. Thing? Hello?

1136
00:49:59,029 --> 00:50:00,597
- [Tom] Doh!

1137
00:50:00,597 --> 00:50:02,199
(Jan laughing)

1138
00:50:02,199 --> 00:50:05,202
- If I had sides, they'd
be splitting right now.

1139
00:50:07,170 --> 00:50:09,473
I'm sorry. It was
just really funny.

1140
00:50:11,742 --> 00:50:13,643
- [Tom] Getting
her juice up there.

1141
00:50:13,643 --> 00:50:15,545
- [Mike] Ew, she's
getting cradle cap.

1142
00:50:18,382 --> 00:50:20,951
- What's locked
behind that door?

1143
00:50:20,951 --> 00:50:24,354
- [Tom] I don't know, what's
locked behind that door?

1144
00:50:24,354 --> 00:50:26,623
- No normal mind could imagine.

1145
00:50:26,623 --> 00:50:28,925
Something even more
terrible than you.

1146
00:50:28,925 --> 00:50:29,893
- Oh, and our bicycles.

1147
00:50:29,893 --> 00:50:33,230
- No, my deformed friend.

1148
00:50:33,230 --> 00:50:38,168
Like all quantities, horror
has its ultimate, and I'm that.

1149
00:50:40,404 --> 00:50:42,806
- There is a horror
beyond yours.

1150
00:50:42,806 --> 00:50:44,174
And it's in there.

1151
00:50:44,174 --> 00:50:45,142
- [Mike] It's Pauly Shore.
(Tom gasping)

1152
00:50:45,142 --> 00:50:46,443
- Locked behind that door.

1153
00:50:46,443 --> 00:50:48,245
- Beside which Carol
Merrill is standing.

1154
00:50:49,546 --> 00:50:51,681
- Miles of experimentation
twist and turn

1155
00:50:51,681 --> 00:50:53,383
through mountains
of miscalculation

1156
00:50:53,383 --> 00:50:56,787
and often lose themselves
in error and darkness.

1157
00:50:56,787 --> 00:50:58,255
- [Mike] And sometimes
things just screw up.

1158
00:50:58,255 --> 00:51:01,224
- Behind that door
is the sum total

1159
00:51:01,224 --> 00:51:02,692
of Dr. Cortner's mistakes.

1160
00:51:02,692 --> 00:51:03,960
- A guy named Murray.

1161
00:51:05,095 --> 00:51:07,364
- [Tom] Well, I gotta
roll. I'll see ya.

1162
00:51:07,364 --> 00:51:09,966
- He had no right to
bring me back to this.

1163
00:51:11,134 --> 00:51:13,470
- [Tom] Boy, you have a
chip on your shoulder.

1164
00:51:13,470 --> 00:51:14,738
Ooh, did it again.

1165
00:51:14,738 --> 00:51:15,572
- Who knows?

1166
00:51:16,473 --> 00:51:17,774
But you should know.

1167
00:51:17,774 --> 00:51:19,743
- This operation's gonna
cost an arm and a leg.

1168
00:51:19,743 --> 00:51:20,944
- [Tom] Zing! Ooh!

1169
00:51:20,944 --> 00:51:22,612
- The doctor injected
a serum into that.

1170
00:51:22,612 --> 00:51:25,148
It was but a mass
of grafted tissues.

1171
00:51:25,148 --> 00:51:26,349
Lifeless.

1172
00:51:27,784 --> 00:51:30,220
It just lay there, weighted
down with its transplants

1173
00:51:30,220 --> 00:51:32,355
of broken limbs
and amputated arms.

1174
00:51:33,457 --> 00:51:36,193
But with this serum-
- I thee wed.

1175
00:51:36,193 --> 00:51:37,994
- It, it began to breathe.

1176
00:51:37,994 --> 00:51:39,196
- That's impossible.

1177
00:51:39,196 --> 00:51:41,631
- [Tom] You got a
lozenge or something?

1178
00:51:41,631 --> 00:51:43,867
- Would you have thought
possible what he's already done?

1179
00:51:43,867 --> 00:51:45,802
- He's worn plaid with stripes?

1180
00:51:45,802 --> 00:51:46,670
- You yourself.

1181
00:51:46,670 --> 00:51:48,071
- [Mike] Spock.

1182
00:51:48,071 --> 00:51:49,573
- He's brought you back.

1183
00:51:49,573 --> 00:51:50,407
You live.

1184
00:51:50,407 --> 00:51:52,309
- [Crow] You live, Joe.

1185
00:51:52,309 --> 00:51:54,511
- Only a few years ago.

1186
00:51:54,511 --> 00:51:56,646
- [Tom] Whoa, hey,
watch the table there.

1187
00:51:56,646 --> 00:51:58,381
Neck juice all over the floor.

1188
00:51:58,381 --> 00:52:00,484
- That's what he's
been doing up here

1189
00:52:00,484 --> 00:52:02,419
where no one would see his work.

1190
00:52:02,419 --> 00:52:03,653
- Yes.

1191
00:52:03,653 --> 00:52:05,355
- That and whoring
at the Moulin Rouge.

1192
00:52:05,355 --> 00:52:08,158
- Experimenting with
transplants on that.

1193
00:52:08,158 --> 00:52:08,992
And on me.

1194
00:52:10,961 --> 00:52:14,764
That he can tear away my
flesh time after time,

1195
00:52:14,764 --> 00:52:16,666
test after test.

1196
00:52:16,666 --> 00:52:18,401
- [Mike] But any job
has its downside.

1197
00:52:18,401 --> 00:52:20,837
- My hopes shattering
with each grafted arm

1198
00:52:20,837 --> 00:52:21,938
he fastened to me.

1199
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:26,176
Watching it wither and warp.

1200
00:52:26,176 --> 00:52:28,278
- [Tom] Why he gave my hand
rich Corinthian leather

1201
00:52:28,278 --> 00:52:29,880
I'll never know.

1202
00:52:29,880 --> 00:52:32,849
- Look, you know they
say there's always
someone worse off?

1203
00:52:32,849 --> 00:52:34,084
I'm that person.

1204
00:52:34,084 --> 00:52:38,021
- You see, he's learned
from his mistakes.

1205
00:52:38,021 --> 00:52:39,156
- [Mike] Guys, I
think he likes her.

1206
00:52:39,156 --> 00:52:40,690
I think he's gonna ask her out.

1207
00:52:40,690 --> 00:52:42,325
- [Tom] Ooh!

1208
00:52:42,325 --> 00:52:46,062
- And you stayed with
him, helping him in
his grotesque work

1209
00:52:46,062 --> 00:52:47,864
that he claims is for science.

1210
00:52:47,864 --> 00:52:50,100
- Hey, you were gonna
marry him, okay?

1211
00:52:50,100 --> 00:52:51,635
- There's no place
for me on the outside.

1212
00:52:51,635 --> 00:52:56,606
This, a world where eyes
would look upon me with pity

1213
00:52:57,807 --> 00:53:01,111
and people would turn
away from me in disgust.

1214
00:53:01,111 --> 00:53:02,879
- [Mike] And then I'd show
them my hand. Thank you!

1215
00:53:02,879 --> 00:53:04,181
It's a one-arm joke.

1216
00:53:05,415 --> 00:53:06,983
- The alcoholic has his bottle.

1217
00:53:06,983 --> 00:53:08,552
- [Tom] Uh, yeah, right he does.

1218
00:53:08,552 --> 00:53:10,387
- The dope addict his needle.

1219
00:53:10,387 --> 00:53:13,323
- The chocoholic his Godivas.

1220
00:53:13,323 --> 00:53:14,791
- I had my research.

1221
00:53:14,791 --> 00:53:17,027
- [Mike] And I had my body.
Could you change my neck juice?

1222
00:53:17,027 --> 00:53:19,529
- To be a surgeon,
it was my life.

1223
00:53:21,665 --> 00:53:25,969
Then one night in
the laboratory, there
was an accident.

1224
00:53:25,969 --> 00:53:27,571
- [Tom] I wet 'em.

1225
00:53:32,108 --> 00:53:33,510
- I have a power.

1226
00:53:34,644 --> 00:53:36,046
This liquid that
he's pumped into me.

1227
00:53:36,046 --> 00:53:37,414
- [Mike] Does it
have NutraSweet?

1228
00:53:37,414 --> 00:53:38,815
- My brain burns with it.

1229
00:53:38,815 --> 00:53:41,117
- Paul Newman's
Sockarooni sauce.

1230
00:53:41,117 --> 00:53:43,520
- That thing inside
and I are in touch.

1231
00:53:43,520 --> 00:53:45,555
Want me to prove it?

1232
00:53:45,555 --> 00:53:47,123
- You can prove nothing.

1233
00:53:48,158 --> 00:53:49,392
You're powerless.

1234
00:53:50,794 --> 00:53:53,663
- I'll show you
how powerless I am.

1235
00:53:53,663 --> 00:53:55,131
- [Tom] Alakazam, huzzah!

1236
00:53:55,131 --> 00:53:55,966
Oh, well.

1237
00:53:57,133 --> 00:53:57,934
- You.

1238
00:53:59,169 --> 00:54:00,303
Behind that door.

